            <ArticleTitle>Intravascular ultrasound evaluation of ruptured plaque in the left main coronary artery misinterpreted as an aneurysm by angiography.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>314-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a patient who demonstrated a left main coronary aneurysm by angiography. Intravascular ultrasound (IVUS) revealed that it was in fact an ulceration, which indicated ruptured plaque. This case provides evidence that IVUS can permit a more powerful definition of ruptured plaque than angiography. On IVUS, ulceration exhibits significantly different characteristics from aneurysm.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
